%% Chapter 41: Error Recovery in Parsing
\label{ch:error-recovery}

\section{Introduction to Error Recovery}
\label{sec:error-recovery-intro}

No parser, no matter how well-designed, can avoid encountering syntax errors. Real-world source code contains errors---from incomplete edits to typos to fundamental misunderstandings of the language syntax. A production-quality parser must not only detect errors but also \emph{recover} from them, allowing the compiler to continue parsing and report multiple errors in a single compilation.

Error recovery is one of the most challenging aspects of parser design. The fundamental tension is between \emph{accuracy} (correctly identifying the location and nature of the error) and \emph{robustness} (continuing to parse after the error without producing spurious error messages). Good error recovery is essential for compiler usability, IDE support, and language tooling.

\begin{historical}
Error recovery in parsers has been studied since the early days of compiler construction. The panic mode recovery strategy was described by earlier practitioners and formalized in textbooks by Aho, Sethi, and Ullman (1986). Phrase-level recovery was developed by Fischer (1973) and further refined by Burke and Fisher (1984). The work on error-correcting parsers by Hendrix (1979) and later by van Zaanen (2003) explored the idea of finding the nearest valid program. Modern work on error-tolerant parsing for IDEs, particularly the DMS Software Reengineering Toolkit (Schwarz, 1990s) and the Tree-sitter parser (Max Brunsfeld, 2015), has pushed the state of the art in practical error recovery.
\end{historical}

\section{Panic Mode Recovery}
\label{sec:panic-mode}

\subsection{Basic Panic Mode}
\label{sec:basic-panic}

\emph{Panic mode} is the simplest error recovery strategy. When an error is detected, the parser discards input tokens until it finds a \emph{synchronizing token}---a token that is likely to begin a new syntactic construct.

\begin{definition}[Panic Mode Recovery]
\label{def:panic-mode}
When an error is detected:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Report the error
  \item Discard input tokens until a \emph{synchronizing token} is found
  \item Resume parsing from the synchronizing token
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Choosing Synchronizing Tokens}
\label{sec:sync-tokens}

The choice of synchronizing tokens is crucial for effective panic mode recovery:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Statement boundaries}: Semicolons, closing braces, and keywords that begin statements (\texttt{if}, \texttt{while}, \texttt{return}) are good synchronizing tokens.
  \item \textbf{Follow sets}: The Follow set of the nonterminal on top of the stack provides a set of tokens that can legally follow it.
  \item \textbf{Language-specific heuristics}: For C-like languages, \texttt{\}}, \texttt{;}, and statement keywords are effective synchronizers.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Panic Mode in Action]
\label{ex:panic-mode}
Consider the input:
\begin{verbatim}
  int x = 5
  if (x > 3) {
    y = x + 1;
  }
\end{verbatim}

Missing semicolon after \texttt{5}. The parser detects an error at \texttt{if}. Using \texttt{if} as a synchronizing token, the parser:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Reports: ``error: expected ';' before 'if'``
  \item Resumes parsing from \texttt{if}
  \item Successfully parses the rest of the program
\end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\subsection{Limitations of Panic Mode}
\label{sec:panic-limits}

Panic mode has several limitations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Lost tokens}: Discarded tokens are not analyzed, so errors within them are missed.
  \item \textbf{Cascading errors}: If the synchronizing token is not found quickly, large portions of the input may be skipped.
  \item \textbf{Inaccurate error location}: The reported error location may be far from the actual error.
\end{itemize}

\section{Phrase-Level Recovery}
\label{sec:phrase-level}

\subsection{Basic Idea}
\label{sec:phrase-level-idea}

\emph{Phrase-level recovery} attempts to make minimal changes to the input or the parser state to continue parsing. Instead of discarding tokens, it inserts, deletes, or replaces tokens to ``fix'' the input.

\begin{definition}[Phrase-Level Recovery]
\label{def:phrase-level}
When an error is detected in state $s$ with lookahead $a$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Design a a set of recovery functions for state $s$, each specifying:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Which tokens to insert, delete, or replace
      \item How to modify the parser state
    \end{itemize}
  \item Apply the recovery function that makes the smallest change
  \item Continue parsing
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Token Insertion}
\label{sec:token-insertion}

\emph{Token insertion} adds a missing token to the input stream. For example, if the parser expects a semicolon but finds a keyword, it can insert a semicolon:

\begin{verbatim}
  State: expecting ';'
  Lookahead: 'if'
  Recovery: insert ';' before 'if'
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Token Deletion}
\label{sec:token-deletion}

\emph{Token deletion} removes an unexpected token from the input stream. For example, if the parser finds an extra closing parenthesis:

\begin{verbatim}
  State: expecting expression
  Lookahead: ')'
  Recovery: delete ')' and continue
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Token Replacement}
\label{sec:token-replacement}

\emph{Token replacement} substitutes one token for another. For example, replacing \texttt{=} with \texttt{==} in a conditional expression:

\begin{verbatim}
  State: expecting '=='
  Lookahead: '='
  Recovery: replace '=' with '=='
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{The Burke--Fisher Algorithm}
\label{sec:burke-fisher}

Burke and Fisher (1984) developed a sophisticated phrase-level recovery algorithm for LR parsers that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Maintains a stack of parser states
  \item At each error, tries a set of local fixes (insertions and deletions)
  \item Evaluates each fix by simulating the parser for a fixed number of steps
  \item Selects the fix that allows the parser to advance the farthest without error
\end{enumerate}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Phrase-level recovery for LR parser}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Let $s$ be the current state and $a$ the lookahead
\For{each candidate fix $f$ (insert token, delete token)}
  \State Simulate parser with fix $f$ for $k$ steps
  \State Record the number of successful steps
\EndFor
\State Select the fix $f^*$ that maximizes successful steps
\If{$f^*$ achieves at least $k$ successful steps}
  \State Apply $f^*$ and continue parsing
\Else
  \State Fall back to panic mode
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Error Productions}
\label{sec:error-productions}

\subsection{Explicit Error Handling in Grammars}
\label{sec:explicit-error}

\emph{Error productions} are grammar rules that explicitly handle common error patterns. The special terminal \texttt{error} matches any token and is used to specify recovery behavior.

\begin{grammar}{Error Productions}
  stmt & \longrightarrow \textbf{error} ; \mid \textbf{error} \} \\
       & \mid \textbf{if} ( \textbf{error} ) stmt \\
       & \mid \textbf{while} ( \textbf{error} ) stmt
\end{grammar}

\subsection{How Error Productions Work}
\label{sec:error-prod-work}

When the parser encounters an error:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item It checks if an error production can be applied in the current state
  \item If so, it shifts the \texttt{error} token, consuming input tokens until a token is found that can follow the error production
  \item The parser then continues normally from the state after the error production
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Advantages and Disadvantages}
\label{sec:error-prod-pros-cons}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Advantages}: Precise control over error recovery; can handle specific error patterns; integrates naturally with LR parsing.
  \item \textbf{Disadvantages}: Requires anticipating common errors; can make the grammar harder to read; may not cover all error cases.
\end{itemize}

\section{Automatic Error Recovery in LR Parsers}
\label{sec:auto-recovery}

\subsection{Minimum Distance Recovery}
\label{sec:min-distance}

The \emph{minimum distance} criterion selects the recovery that requires the fewest changes to the input:

\begin{definition}[Minimum Distance Recovery]
\label{def:min-distance}
Among all possible recoveries (insertions, deletions, replacements), select the one that minimizes the total number of changes to the input string.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Local Error Correction}
\label{sec:local-correction}

\emph{Local error correction} tries all possible single-token changes (insert, delete, replace) and selects the one that allows the parser to advance the farthest:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\For{each token $t$ in the alphabet}
  \State Try inserting $t$ before the current position
  \State Simulate parsing; record distance
\EndFor
\State Try deleting the current token
\State Simulate parsing; record distance
\For{each token $t$ in the alphabet}
  \State Try replacing current token with $t$
  \State Simulate parsing; record distance
\EndFor
\State Select the change with maximum distance
\end{algorithmic}

\section{Error-Correcting Parsers}
\label{sec:error-correcting}

\subsection{The Idea of Error Correction}
\label{sec:ec-idea}

An \emph{error-correcting parser} treats parsing as a search problem: find the ``nearest'' valid program to the input and parse that. The distance between the input and the corrected program is measured by the number of insertions, deletions, and replacements.

\begin{definition}[Error-Correcting Parser]
\label{def:ec-parser}
An \textbf{error-correcting parser} for a grammar $G$ takes an input string $w$ and produces a parse tree for the string $w'$ that minimizes the edit distance $d(w, w')$ subject to $w' \in L(G)$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Complexity of Error Correction}
\label{sec:ec-complexity}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Error Correction]
\label{thm:ec-complexity}
Finding the minimum-distance correction for a general CFG is NP-hard in the worst case. However, for a fixed maximum correction distance $k$, the problem can be solved in $O(n \cdot |G|^{O(k)})$ time.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Practical Error-Correcting Parsers}
\label{sec:ec-practical}

Several practical error-correcting parsers have been developed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{DMS Error Recovery} (Schwarz): Uses a combination of local correction and global analysis.
  \item \textbf{Adaptive LR} (Grune and Sellier): Extends LR parsing with on-the-fly grammar modification.
  \item \textbf{Error-Recovery GLR} (van Zaanen): Extends GLR parsing with error correction.
\end{itemize}

\section{Modern Approaches: Error-Tolerant Parsing for IDEs}
\label{sec:modern-approaches}

\subsection{Requirements for IDE Parsing}
\label{sec:ide-requirements}

IDEs have different parsing requirements than compilers:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Incremental parsing}: The parser must handle small edits efficiently, re-parsing only the changed portion.
  \item \textbf{Error tolerance}: The parser must produce a useful syntax tree even when the code contains errors.
  \item \textbf{Speed}: Parsing must be fast enough for real-time feedback (typically under 100ms).
  \item \textbf{Partial results}: The parser must produce results for the portions of the file that are valid, even if other portions have errors.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Tree-sitter}
\label{sec:tree-sitter}

\emph{Tree-sitter} (Brunsfeld, 2015) is a modern parser generator designed for IDE use. Its key innovations include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GLR-based parsing}: Uses GLR parsing to handle ambiguous grammars.
  \item \textbf{Error recovery by insertion}: When an error is encountered, Tree-sitter inserts an \texttt{ERROR} node and attempts to continue parsing.
  \item \textbf{Incremental parsing}: Maintains a concrete syntax tree that can be updated incrementally as the user edits the source.
  \item \textbf{Automatic token recovery}: Automatically tries inserting or deleting tokens to continue parsing.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Tree-sitter Error Node]
\label{def:tree-sitter-error}
An \textbf{ERROR node} in a Tree-sitter syntax tree represents a portion of the input that could not be parsed. The ERROR node contains the tokens that could not be incorporated into the tree, allowing the rest of the tree to remain valid.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Comparison of Error Recovery Strategies}
\label{sec:comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
