\subsection{Best Practices for Error Recovery}
\label{sec:best-practices}

Based on experience with production compilers and IDEs, the following best practices have emerged:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Use multiple strategies}: Combine panic mode (for gross errors) with phrase-level recovery (for local errors).
  \item \textbf{Limit recovery scope}: Don't try to recover too aggressively; sometimes it's better to skip to the next statement.
  \item \textbf{Provide good error messages}: Include the expected tokens, the actual token, and the source location.
  \item \textbf{Avoid cascading errors}: After an error, suppress related errors that are likely consequences of the first error.
  \item \textbf{Test with real error patterns}: Error recovery should be tested with realistic error patterns from actual users.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:error-recovery-summary}

Error recovery is a critical component of production-quality parsers. The key approaches include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Panic mode recovery: Simple but limited; discards tokens until a synchronizing token is found
  \item Phrase-level recovery: More precise; makes minimal changes to the input or parser state
  \item Error productions: Explicit grammar rules for handling common error patterns
  \item Error-correcting parsers: Find the nearest valid program to the input
  \item Modern IDE-oriented approaches: Tree-sitter and similar tools that combine GLR parsing with incremental, error-tolerant parsing
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Error recovery has evolved from simple panic mode strategies to sophisticated, IDE-oriented approaches. Key milestones include: panic mode recovery (1960s--1970s); phrase-level recovery (Fischer, 1973); Burke--Fisher algorithm (1984); error-correcting parsers (Hendrix, 1979); DMS error recovery (Schwarz, 1990s); Tree-sitter (Brunsfeld, 2015); and ongoing work on error-tolerant parsing for language servers and IDEs. The development of Tree-sitter has been particularly influential, demonstrating that high-quality error recovery can be achieved automatically through GLR parsing and incremental tree updates.
\end{historical}

