\section{Error-Correcting Parsers: Advanced Topics}
\label{sec:error-correcting-advanced}

\subsection{Edit Distance Metrics for Parsing}
\label{sec:edit-distance-parsing}

The error-correcting parser finds the nearest valid program by minimizing an edit distance. Different distance metrics lead to different corrections:

\begin{definition}[Edit Distance for Parsing]
\label{def:edit-distance}
Given an input string $w$ and a grammar $G$, the \textbf{edit distance} $d(w, L(G))$ is:
\[
d(w, L(G)) = \min_{w' \in L(G)} d(w, w')
\]
where $d(w, w')$ is the Levenshtein edit distance (minimum number of insertions, deletions, and substitutions to transform $w$ into $w'$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Weighted Edit Distance]
\label{def:weighted-edit}
A \textbf{weighted edit distance} assigns different costs to different operations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Insertion of token $t$: cost $c_i(t)$
  \item Deletion of token $t$: cost $c_d(t)$
  \item Substitution of $t_1$ for $t_2$: cost $c_s(t_1, t_2)$
\end{itemize}
This allows the parser to prefer likely corrections (e.g., inserting a missing semicolon is cheaper than deleting an entire statement).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Earley-Based Error Correction}
\label{sec:earley-correction}

The Earley parser can be extended to support error correction by allowing \emph{error transitions} in the parsing automaton:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Parse with error correction}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Initialize Earley sets $S_0, S_1, \ldots$
\For{each input position $i$}
  \State \textbf{Prediction}: Add predicted items as usual
  \State \textbf{Scanning}: For each active item expecting token $a$:
    \If{$\text{input}[i] = a$}
      \State Complete the scan normally
    \Else
      \State \textbf{Error transitions}:
      \For{each token $t$ in the alphabet}
        \State Try \emph{inserting} $t$ before position $i$ (cost $c_i(t)$)
        \State Try \emph{substituting} $t$ for $\text{input}[i]$ (cost $c_s(t, \text{input}[i])$)
      \EndFor
      \State Try \emph{deleting} $\text{input}[i]$ (cost $c_d(\text{input}[i])$)
    \EndIf
  \State \textbf{Completion}: Complete items as usual
  \State Select the parse with minimum total correction cost
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Error-Correcting Earley]
\label{thm:ec-earley}
The error-correcting Earley parser runs in $O(n^3 \cdot |\Sigma|)$ time for a single-error correction at each position, where $|\Sigma|$ is the alphabet size. For a maximum correction distance $k$, the complexity is $O(n^3 \cdot |\Sigma|^k)$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Practical Error-Correcting Parsers}
\label{sec:ec-practical-advanced}

Several practical systems implement error-correcting parsing:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{DMS Error Recovery}: Uses a combination of local correction (trying single-token changes) and global analysis (checking that the correction leads to a valid parse). The DMS system can handle arbitrary CFGs and provides guaranteed error correction within a configurable distance bound.
  \item \textbf{Napier's Error-Correcting Parser}: Uses a priority queue to explore corrections in order of increasing cost, ensuring that the minimum-cost correction is found first.
  \item \textbf{Hutton's Approach}: Extends parser combinators with error correction by wrapping each parser with a recovery combinator that tries insertions and deletions.
\end{itemize}

\section{IDE Integration: Detailed Treatment}
\label{sec:ide-integration-detailed}

\subsection{Incremental Parsing Architecture}
\label{sec:incremental-architecture}

Modern IDEs require parsers that can handle continuous editing. The architecture typically involves:

\begin{definition}[Incremental Parse Tree]
\label{def:incremental-tree}
An \textbf{incremental parse tree} is a concrete syntax tree that supports:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Edit application}: Given a text edit (insert, delete, replace at position $p$), update the tree efficiently.
  \item \textbf{Re-parsing}: Re-parse only the portion of the tree affected by the edit.
  \item \textbf{Stable node identity}: Nodes that are not affected by the edit retain their identity, allowing IDE features (selection, highlighting) to remain stable.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Update parse tree after an edit}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Apply text edit to the source buffer
\State Identify the \emph{edit region} $[p, p + \Delta]$
\State Find the deepest tree node that fully contains the edit region
\State \textbf{Invalidate} this node and all its ancestors' cached parse state
\State Re-lex the edit region (and a small context window)
\State Re-parse from the start of the invalidated node
\State Stop when the parser reaches a \emph{recovery point}:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item A position where the new parse matches the old parse's state
    \item A position beyond the edit region where the old tree is still valid
  \end{itemize}
\State Splice the new subtree into the old tree at the recovery point
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental Parsing Efficiency]
\label{thm:incremental-parse}
For a local edit (changing $k$ characters), incremental parsing takes $O(k + d)$ time, where $d$ is the distance to the recovery point. In the common case, $d$ is small (a few tokens), making the update effectively $O(k)$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Tree-sitter's Error Recovery in Detail}
\label{sec:tree-sitter-detail}

Tree-sitter's error recovery combines several strategies:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Error node insertion}: When the parser cannot match any token, it creates an \texttt{ERROR} node containing the unmatched token.
  \item \textbf{Token insertion}: The parser tries inserting expected tokens (based on the current grammar state) to see if parsing can continue.
  \item \textbf{Token deletion}: The parser tries skipping the current token to see if the next token can be matched.
  \item \textbf{Token replacement}: The parser tries replacing the current token with an expected token.
  \item \textbf{Best recovery selection}: Among all possible recoveries, the parser selects the one that allows it to consume the most tokens before the next error.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Tree-sitter Recovery Score]
\label{def:recovery-score}
The \textbf{recovery score} of a recovery action is the number of tokens successfully consumed after the recovery. The parser selects the recovery with the highest score. Ties are broken by preferring insertions over deletions over replacements.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Language Server Protocol Integration}
\label{sec:lsp-integration}

The Language Server Protocol (LSP) defines a standard interface between IDEs and language tools. Error-tolerant parsers are essential for LSP implementations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Diagnostics}: The parser produces error diagnostics (position, message, severity) for display in the IDE.
  \item \textbf{Semantic tokens}: The parser produces a syntax tree that enables semantic highlighting even in the presence of errors.
  \item \textbf{Code completion}: The parser provides the context (current scope, expected tokens) for code completion suggestions.
  \item \textbf{Go to definition}: The parser resolves name references to their declarations, even in partially valid code.
  \item \textbf{Refactoring}: The parser provides a syntax tree that can be transformed, even when parts of the code have errors.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Error-Tolerant Code Completion]
\label{ex:error-tolerant-completion}
Consider the following incomplete code:
\begin{verbatim}
  function calculateTotal(items: Item[]): number {
    let total = 0;
    for (const item of items) {
      total += item.  // cursor here
    }
    return total;
  }
\end{verbatim}
The parser encounters an incomplete expression (\texttt{item.}) but can still:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Determine that the cursor is inside a member access expression
  \item Resolve \texttt{item} to the loop variable of type \texttt{Item}
  \item Provide completion suggestions for members of \texttt{Item}
\end{itemize}
This requires the parser to produce a meaningful tree even with the incomplete expression.
\end{example}

\subsection{Fuzzy Parsing}
\label{sec:fuzzy-parsing}

\emph{Fuzzy parsing} goes beyond error recovery by attempting to extract structure from severely malformed input:

\begin{definition}[Fuzzy Parsing]
\label{def:fuzzy-parsing}
\textbf{Fuzzy parsing} attempts to identify syntactic constructs in input that is not valid according to the grammar, using heuristics and approximate matching. It produces a ``best effort'' parse tree that captures as much structure as possible.
\end{definition}

Fuzzy parsing is used in:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Code search tools}: Finding code patterns across large codebases, even in partially valid files.
  \item \textbf{Refactoring tools}: Identifying code structure for transformation, even when the code has errors.
  \item \textbf{Documentation generators}: Extracting function signatures and types from source code for documentation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Formal Properties of Error Recovery}
\label{sec:formal-error-recovery}

\subsection{Correctness Criteria}
\label{sec:correctness-criteria}

\begin{definition}[Error Recovery Correctness]
\label{def:er-correctness}
An error recovery strategy is \textbf{correct} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Soundness}: Every token in the recovered parse tree corresponds to a token in the original input (no spurious tokens are introduced, except through explicit insertion).
  \item \textbf{Completeness}: Every non-error node in the parse tree is a valid subtree according to the grammar.
  \item \textbf{Minimality}: The number of error nodes is minimized (or bounded by the edit distance to the nearest valid program).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Tree-sitter Correctness]
\label{thm:tree-sitter-correct}
Tree-sitter's error recovery satisfies soundness and completeness. Minimality is approximated by the recovery score heuristic but is not guaranteed in general.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Cascading Error Suppression}
\label{sec:cascading-suppression}

A major challenge in error recovery is \emph{cascading errors}: a single error can cause many spurious error messages as the parser struggles to resynchronize.

\begin{definition}[Error Recovery Region]
\label{def:error-region}
An \textbf{error recovery region} is a contiguous portion of the input where the parser is in recovery mode. Within a recovery region, additional errors are suppressed (or reported at a lower severity) to avoid cascading error messages.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Suppress cascading errors}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\text{in\_recovery} \gets \text{false}$
\State $\text{recovery\_start} \gets -1$
\For{each parse event}
  \If{error detected}
    \If{$\text{in\_recovery}$}
      \State Suppress error (already in recovery region)
    \Else
      \State Report error
      \State $\text{in\_recovery} \gets \text{true}$
      \State $\text{recovery\_start} \gets \text{current position}$
    \EndIf
  \ElsIf{$\text{in\_recovery}$ and parser has advanced $k$ tokens without error}
    \State $\text{in\_recovery} \gets \text{false}$ \Comment{Recovery succeeded}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
